const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Clinical Literature Review Service","serviceType":"Systematic, rapid, scoping and regulatory evidence synthesis for clinical and regulatory use","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/physician-writing-services/clinical-literature-review-for-an-evidence-based-medicine/","description":"Systematic, rapid, scoping and regulatory literature reviews: protocol registered before screening, search reported so it can be re-run.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the difference between a systematic review and a clinical literature review?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“Clinical literature review” is an umbrella term; “systematic review” is a specific method with conditions attached. A systematic review answers a pre-defined question using a protocol written and registered before the search, a search reported in enough detail for somebody else to run it, screening by two people independently, formal risk-of-bias assessment and a certainty rating. A clinical literature review may be any of six things: systematic, rapid, scoping, umbrella, narrative or a targeted review written into a regulatory submission. Each is legitimate under its own name. The problem is only ever the mismatch — a narrative review given a flow diagram, a scoping review asked for an effect estimate, a single-reviewer review called systematic. What makes a review systematic is not the number of databases; it is that the protocol existed before the evidence did."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I conduct a clinical literature review for evidence-based medicine?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In this order, and the order is the method. Frame the question so it can be searched — PICO for effect questions, SPIDER for qualitative, PIRD for diagnostic accuracy. Decide which review type the question and your timeline support. Write a protocol to PRISMA-P and register it on PROSPERO before you screen anything. Build the search in each database’s own vocabulary and test it against studies you already know belong in the review. Screen with two people independently, recording every full-text exclusion with its reason. Extract into a form you piloted first, with outcome data in duplicate. Appraise with the instrument that fits the studies. Then synthesise — narratively or by meta-analysis, decided from what the studies support — and grade certainty per outcome. Running these out of order is what quietly destroys the claim: adjusting inclusion criteria once the results are visible produces a perfectly readable review that no longer supports the thing it says about itself."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I have to register the protocol?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"For a systematic review, yes in practice, and before screening begins rather than before submission. Registration is what supports every claim the review makes about its own method: that the question was fixed, that the inclusion criteria were not adjusted as the results appeared, that the outcomes reported are the ones planned and in the order planned. The registration date is permanently visible in PROSPERO, so a review registered after screening started is not making a smaller claim — it is making the same claim with the support removed, and reviewers check. Practical notes: PROSPERO takes systematic, rapid and umbrella reviews but not scoping reviews, which go to the Open Science Framework instead. Deviating from a registered protocol is entirely normal and happens in most reviews; what matters is reporting each deviation with its reason rather than quietly updating the record."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which tools should I use to appraise the studies?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The instrument follows the studies being appraised, not the review doing the appraising. Randomised trials: RoB 2, judged per outcome rather than per study, because the same trial can be at low risk for mortality and high risk for a subjective outcome. Non-randomised studies of interventions: ROBINS-I, and state which version, because ROBINS-I V2 has circulated as a draft and is not yet settled. Exposure studies: ROBINS-E. Diagnostic accuracy: QUADAS-3, which superseded QUADAS-2 in February 2026 — a review submitted now using QUADAS-2 will be asked about it. Systematic reviews, in an umbrella review: AMSTAR 2. Qualitative research: a CASP checklist, with GRADE-CERQual for confidence in the findings. What we do not use is the Jadad scale, which is still listed on a great many review pages. Cochrane advises against summary quality scores, because collapsing several domains into one number hides which domain was the problem, and domain-based assessment replaced them more than a decade ago."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How many databases do I need?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Fewer than people think, and chosen rather than counted. For a review of randomised evidence in a biomedical field, MEDLINE, Embase and the Cochrane Library cover most of it, with trial registries for completed-but-unpublished trials. Scopus or Web of Science add coverage outside the biomedical core and let you chase citations forward. CINAHL matters for nursing and allied health, PsycINFO for mental health, and regional databases — LILACS, African Journals Online, CNKI — matter far more than most English-language reviews acknowledge. What does not improve a review is adding a sixth database to the list and running the same badly translated strategy in it. Two well-built searches retrieve more than five pasted ones, because the vocabularies genuinely differ: MeSH in MEDLINE and Emtree in Embase are different thesauri, and a MEDLINE strategy pasted into Embase returns plausible-looking results from the wrong set. Report what you searched and, just as importantly, what you did not and why."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can one person do a systematic review?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"One person can do an excellent review, but not one that meets the usual conditions for calling it systematic, because independent dual screening is one of them. Single-reviewer screening misses eligible studies at a rate that is well documented and not small, and the misses are not random — they cluster in the records that are ambiguous from the abstract, which are disproportionately the interesting ones. There are two honest routes. Do it as a rapid review and say in the methods exactly which systematic-review steps were abbreviated and why, which is a respected and widely published form. Or bring in a second screener for the screening stage only, which is the cheapest part of the review to buy and the one where a second pair of eyes changes the result. What does not work is single screening described in a way that leaves a reader to assume otherwise."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What if the search returns almost nothing?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"First check that it is the field and not the strategy, which is why testing against known studies before running matters. If the search is sound and the field really is thin, that is a finding rather than a failure, and it is often a more useful one than the review you were expecting. A well-documented empty or near-empty review tells a guideline panel that no recommendation can be made, tells a funder exactly where the gap is, and tells a trialist what the next study should be. Empty reviews are published and are cited. What does not work is widening the inclusion criteria until something appears, because the criteria were registered, the change is visible, and a review whose scope expanded after the results were seen cannot claim what a systematic review claims. Where we find this we will say so and set out the honest options: report the gap, reframe the question deliberately and re-register, or stop."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is a regulatory literature review the same as a systematic review?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No, and treating them as the same is expensive in both directions. A literature review inside an EU MDR clinical evaluation, an IVDR performance evaluation, an HTA submission or a regulatory dossier is a controlled document within a technical file. It is judged by an auditor or an assessor against the submission’s own template and expectations, it carries a version history rather than a publication date, and it has to be re-runnable to a stated date because the obligation recurs — a clinical evaluation is updated, not finished. It also frequently has to appraise evidence about a specific device or product rather than answer a general effect question, which changes the search entirely. A systematic review written to PRISMA is not wrong in this setting, and PRISMA is genuinely the clearest available structure for reporting the search, but it answers a different question and takes roughly twice as long. Conversely, a regulatory literature review submitted to a journal will be asked why there is no protocol registration."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How long does a systematic review actually take?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Published estimates for academic teams run to a year or more, though much of that is elapsed time rather than work — people screening in the evenings between other commitments. As concentrated work at the scale most clinical reviews run to, budget something like three weeks of reviewer time across all stages, with screening the largest single block and full-text retrieval the step that always takes longer than anyone plans for. The variables that actually move the number are the size of the search, whether screening is dual, how many studies survive to extraction, and whether authors have to be contacted for missing data. The chooser at the top of this page gives an estimate for your parameters, and our free screening workload estimator breaks the same figure down stage by stage with every rate as an editable field, so you can argue with ours. The most reliable way to make a review take too long is a search that returns four thousand records to find forty studies — an hour with somebody who builds searches saves more time than any amount of screening speed."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Physician writing","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/physician-writing-services/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Clinical literature review","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/physician-writing-services/clinical-literature-review-for-an-evidence-based-medicine/"}]}];

